Insight 1: The Target is Highly Skewed and NormalizedThe Data: Your maximum demand is exactly $1.0$, and the minimum is extremely close to zero ($6.2 \times 10^{-7}$). Your skewness is 3.73 and kurtosis is 17.33.The Meaning: The demand has already been scaled (Min-Max scaling) between 0 and 1 by the organizers. Furthermore, the high skewness means most of your data sits very close to 0, but you have occasional, extreme spikes up to 1.0.The Fix: If you feed this directly into a model, it will heavily bias itself toward the low values and fail to predict the spikes. You should apply a log transformation to the target variable during training, specifically $y' = \log1p(y)$ (which is $\log(y + 1)$). This pulls the extreme spikes closer to the median, helping the model learn the patterns better. You must remember to reverse this using expm1() before submitting!
Insight 2: The "Day 48" Sensor FailureThe Data: Day 48 has 2241 missing temperatures. Day 49 only has 254. The missing data is not random; it is highly concentrated on a specific day.The Meaning: Simply filling these missing values with the overall mean (average) temperature will destroy your temporal continuity. If a sensor went down on Day 48 during a rainstorm, inserting the "average sunny temperature" will confuse the model.The Fix: You must sort your data by geohash and timestamp, and then use Forward-Fill (ffill) or Interpolation. This ensures that if the 14:15 temperature is missing, it borrows the temperature from 14:00.
Insight 3: The Cold Start Problem (The Competition Killer)The Data: You have 10 geohashes in the test set that do not exist in the training set.The Meaning: If you use simple One-Hot Encoding or Target Encoding on the geohash string (e.g., converting "gcpv7g" to an ID), your model will encounter these 10 new codes in the test set, panic, and likely output a default or average prediction, tanking your $R^2$ score.The Fix: We must mathematically break the geohash strings down into their core components: Latitude and Longitude. Tree-based models (like XGBoost or LightGBM) are exceptionally good at drawing boundary boxes around Lat/Lon coordinates to group neighboring areas together.